ground \nin search of the Jewel. Your stomach sinks a little bit, and you begin your \nspeech.\n\nYou tell the audience about your travels. You tell them about a disruptive \ngeyser, a flooding river, and a magical coat that saved your skin on more than \none occasion. You tell them about three fearsome dragons and how much you've \nended up regretting ever trespassing in their domains. And last, you tell them \nabout the Jewel. It's just a myth, you say. There is no Jewel, and if there was \none, it was either stolen or is in some other part of the underground. When the \ncollective group hears this, there is a resounding sense of shock and dismay.\n\n\"How could this be?\" starts Allarah. \"Our druids were sure that the Jewel was \nbeing protected by the Red Dragon!\"\n\n\"The Red Dragon is a mistress of trickery, High Priestess. I think she may have \nsent you false information on purpose. The truth is, I found no Jewel.\"\n\nAllarah looks at you incredulously and the throng of people begins to disperse. \nThis effectively ends your hero's welcome. You step down from the podium and \nhead home.\n\n*********\n\nYears later, you find yourself with your own family. You have two children and \na loving spouse. You've also inherited most of the land that your father once \ntilled. You still impress young children with fantastic stories about the \nunderground, but you are by no means regarded as any sort of hero. The \nexpeditions into the Earth's crust have more or less stopped in recent years. \nEvery couple of years a few parties of adventurers attempt to enter the \nunderground in search of the Jewel, but none ever return. This of course only \nincreases public wariness about entering the underground.\n\nOne day, just after the harvest season, you make your way back to the sinkhole \nthat first led you beneath the surface. It is now covered with signs warning \npotential spelunkers about the dangers of cave travelling and the perils that \nawait. You smile briefly